# shir_nodes/cgo_call.node — CGO-PATH expression node.
# A call into a CGO-bound native library (tree-sitter bindings, etc.)
# that has NO shell/JS/Perl semantics: the faithful translation is the
# NATIVE CALL ITSELF, executable only via the C frontend path (the
# program becomes a C-backend build linking the native library).
#
# target: dotted Go callee verbatim ("sitter.NewParser",
#         "parser.Parse", "node.Content", ...)
# args:   evaluated argument expressions
#
# Backend contract:
#   C    : renders the mapped native call (tree-sitter api.h family);
#          UNKNOWN targets render `/* cgo-path: unimplemented <target>
#          */ (assert(0), 0)` — loud, never silent.
#   JS   : sh2.cgoUnsupported("target") — throws at RUNTIME (never
#          silently wrong); browser deployments bundle the WASM/native
#          bridge instead.
#   Perl : die "otranspilerl: cgo-path construct <target> requires the C
#          frontend build\n"

node CgoCall
tag "CgoCall"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field target: string
field args: exprs
# Unstated — the missing component is FOREIGN-CALL MARSHALLING: the C ABI's type mapping,
# ownership and lifetime of returned pointers, the error/errno channel, and the call's
# side effects on process state. The node has no byte-level value contract; what would
# need modelling is a foreign heap plus a marshalling relation, neither of which exists
# here. Recorded as a named missing model rather than the bare word "effect".
# LAW CANDIDATE: `result = marshal_out (C_fn (marshal_in args))`. Stated plainly, this is
# NOT a law of the node: both sides are the FOREIGN function's contract (its return type,
# its ownership rules, its errno channel), and the node only fixes that the call is made
# with those arguments. Any candidate that does not mention the foreign function collapses
# to `result = result`. So the missing component is concrete and named: the C ABI
# MARSHALLING relation (type mapping, ownership/lifetime of returned pointers, the
# errno/error channel) plus a foreign heap — none of which exists here — and no byte-level
# law is claimed meanwhile.
spec: none (unstated: missing model — foreign-call marshalling: C ABI type mapping,
# ownership/lifetime of returned pointers, errno/error channel, foreign heap.)
